   :adr/body "# RFC 9180 HPKE — the whole surface, and what it costs

Asked whether the wolfSSL article's HPKE was implemented here. It is:
`kotoba-lang/org-ietf-hpke`, RFC 9180, portable .cljc. The article enumerates
five KEMs, three KDFs, three AEADs and four modes; this had one of each KEM,
KDF and AEAD, and one mode. The owner then asked for all four gaps: the
remaining modes, the remaining suites, TLS-ECH, and ODoH/MLS.

## The measurement that reordered the work

The claim that stopped the other suites was written into org-ietf-hpke's own
docstring: \"AES-GCM, P-256, P-384, P-521, X448, SHA-384 and SHA-512 are each
an implementation this workspace does not have.\"

Two of those were wrong. `sha2.sha512` has had SHA-384 and SHA-512 since the
Ed25519 work, and `org-nist-sha2`'s README simultaneously documented them and
listed them under \"Not implemented\" — the stale sentence sat three headings
above the section describing the functions. Both files have been corrected.

The rest was measured rather than assumed, and holds:

  AES / AES-GCM     was absent. Every AES in the tree was Node's crypto
                    (kotobase's storage profile and erasure lifecycle).
                    GitHub code search reported nothing, but that index does
                    not cover this org -- controls for `hpke` and `x25519`,
                    both known present, also return zero, so the finding came
                    from reading trees. **Now kotoba-lang/org-nist-aes.**
  P-256/384/521     no portable implementation. btc-crypto has secp256k1
                    point arithmetic, but schnorr.cljc and core.cljc are
                    #?(:clj) only over java.math.BigInteger and the :cljs
                    branch throws. eth-crypto's secp256k1 is .cljs only.
                    Neither is a short-Weierstrass curve with a = -3.
  X448 / Curve448   nothing.
  HMAC-SHA384       was missing. Added.

## What is done

**Four modes, anchored to the RFC rather than to an oracle.** RFC 9180
Appendix A.2 is this library's suite exactly -- DHKEM(X25519, HKDF-SHA256),
HKDF-SHA256, ChaCha20Poly1305 -- and publishes base, PSK, Auth and AuthPSK.
The earlier argument for shipping base alone was that the others would be
untested code paths; that was an argument about evidence, and the evidence
was in the specification.

A.2 is extracted by `scripts/extract_rfc9180.cljs` from a text pinned by
sha256, not transcribed. The repo had already been bitten once: an earlier
suite claimed A.2 vectors and carried A.1's ikmR, caught only by an
assertion on pkRm. 404 assertions on both runtimes, 591 with the oracle.

Discrimination, measured one break at a time: dropping the mode byte from
key_schedule_context turns 245 red across all four modes; swapping pkRm/pkSm
in the auth kem_context turns 64 red and leaves base and PSK **green**;
dropping auth-encap's second DH, the same 64. The localisation is the point
-- it is what makes those numbers evidence about the added code.


**HMAC-SHA-384**, for HKDF-SHA384. The substitution that matters is the key
transformation: HMAC-SHA-384 and HMAC-SHA-512 share a 128-byte block, so
using the wrong hash there changes nothing until a key exceeds 128 bytes.
RFC 4231 case 6 is that key, and it is the single assertion the break turns
red.

**org-nist-aes** -- AES (FIPS 197, forward cipher only) and AES-GCM (SP
800-38D), zero dependencies, seal/open mirroring chacha20.aead so an HPKE
suite can be a parameter rather than a rewrite. Tested against the complete
Wycheproof corpus: 316 cases, every group, no sampling, pinned by sha256 and
re-extractable. 1,368 assertions on both runtimes, 1,667 with a javax.crypto
oracle sweeping message, AAD and IV lengths the fixed corpus does not name.

Two rows of its break table are worth carrying here. Incrementing the whole
128-bit block instead of the low 32 bits turns **54** of 1,368 red -- the
break is invisible to any message short enough not to carry. Comparing only
the first tag byte turns **108** red and **only in the cases that are
supposed to fail**: every correct decryption stays correct, so a suite
without adversarial cases would have called it green.

And one that is not hypothetical: it shipped on the first run with `Rcon[j]`
written as `x^j` instead of `x^(j-1)`. The cipher was still a permutation,
still round-tripped against itself, and agreed with nothing else in the
world -- 866 assertions red. **Deriving a table protects against a mistyped
constant, not against an off-by-one in the index that reads it**, which is a
narrower guarantee than the derive-don't-transcribe habit implies.

## What remains, in dependency order

AES-GCM was the keystone and it has landed, so the ordering below is no
longer blocked at its root. Every named consumer wanted it: ECH configs in
the wild advertise AES-128-GCM, and RFC 9230 names
X25519/HKDF-SHA256/AES-128-GCM as ODoH's mandatory suite. Until it existed
the consumers could only be built against a suite no deployed peer offers.

  1. hpke suite parameters   DONE below the line: AES-GCM exists. kdf_id
                             and aead_id become values rather than
                             constants. Validates against RFC 9180 A.1,
                             which is the same KEM with AES-128-GCM -- the
                             extractor already reads that section. This is
                             the next commit, and it is small.
  3. nist curves             P-256 first (A.3), then P-384/P-521. Needs
                             portable modular arithmetic over a 256-bit
                             prime and, unlike X25519, rejection sampling
                             in DeriveKeyPair.
  4. org-ietf-x448           RFC 7748 Curve448, and DHKEM(X448) (A.6).
  5. TLS-ECH                 into org-ietf-tls, which is a TLS 1.3 client
                             with no ECH today -- measured: no path in its
                             tree matches ech/hpke, and extension.cljc does
                             not carry one.
  6. ODoH (RFC 9230)         HPKE plus a small message format. The cheapest
                             consumer once AES-GCM lands.
  7. MLS (RFC 9420)          largest by far; an independent group key
                             agreement sits on top.

## What this does not claim

Nothing here is deployed. HPKE is a leaf library with no consumer in this
workspace, which is the honest reading of \"implemented\": the primitive is
real and the things RFC 9180 was written for are not built yet.

The BouncyCastle oracle still covers base mode only. A second opinion on
base mode is not a second opinion on auth mode, and the README says so
rather than letting \"differential tested\" spread across all four."}]
